{
	"cell_id": 9028834938521568656,
	"cells": [
		{
			"cell_id": 6051822203420875133,
			"cell_origin": "client",
			"cell_type": "latex",
			"cells": [
				{
					"cell_id": 6051822203420875133,
					"cell_origin": "client",
					"cell_type": "latex_view",
					"source": "\\section*{Local supersymmetry of $N=1$, $d=4$ supergravity}\n\nThis notebook shows that the action of $N=1$ supergravity in four dimensions is\ninvariant under local supersymmetry. It is a more complete version of the example\nin section~3 of hep-th/0701238, and follows the discussion in sections~1.6 and 1.7 of\nP.~van~Nieuwenhuizen, \\emph{Supergravity}, Phys.~Rept.~68 (1981) 189 (referred to as vN below),\nstep by step.\n\nWe use the 1.5 order formalism: the spin connection $\\omega_\\mu{}^{mn}=\\omega_\\mu{}^{mn}(e,\\psi)$\nsolves its own field equation, so its variation never has to be computed and we can set\n$\\delta\\omega=0$. Only the vielbeine and gravitini which appear explicitly in the action are varied.\nConventions: $\\kappa=1$, Pauli metric (so the flat metric is $\\delta_{mn}$), and\n\\begin{equation*}\n\\delta e^m{}_\\mu = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\bar\\epsilon\\Gamma^m\\psi_\\mu\\,,\\qquad\n\\delta\\psi_\\mu = D_\\mu\\epsilon\\,,\\qquad\nD_\\mu\\epsilon = \\partial_\\mu\\epsilon + \\tfrac{1}{4}\\omega_\\mu{}^{mn}\\Gamma_{mn}\\epsilon\\,.\n\\end{equation*}\nThe covariant derivative $D_\\mu$ contains only the spin connection, not a Christoffel\nconnection."
				}
			],
			"hidden": true,
			"source": "\\section*{Local supersymmetry of $N=1$, $d=4$ supergravity}\n\nThis notebook shows that the action of $N=1$ supergravity in four dimensions is\ninvariant under local supersymmetry. It is a more complete version of the example\nin section~3 of hep-th/0701238, and follows the discussion in sections~1.6 and 1.7 of\nP.~van~Nieuwenhuizen, \\emph{Supergravity}, Phys.~Rept.~68 (1981) 189 (referred to as vN below),\nstep by step.\n\nWe use the 1.5 order formalism: the spin connection $\\omega_\\mu{}^{mn}=\\omega_\\mu{}^{mn}(e,\\psi)$\nsolves its own field equation, so its variation never has to be computed and we can set\n$\\delta\\omega=0$. Only the vielbeine and gravitini which appear explicitly in the action are varied.\nConventions: $\\kappa=1$, Pauli metric (so the flat metric is $\\delta_{mn}$), and\n\\begin{equation*}\n\\delta e^m{}_\\mu = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\bar\\epsilon\\Gamma^m\\psi_\\mu\\,,\\qquad\n\\delta\\psi_\\mu = D_\\mu\\epsilon\\,,\\qquad\nD_\\mu\\epsilon = \\partial_\\mu\\epsilon + \\tfrac{1}{4}\\omega_\\mu{}^{mn}\\Gamma_{mn}\\epsilon\\,.\n\\end{equation*}\nThe covariant derivative $D_\\mu$ contains only the spin connection, not a Christoffel\nconnection."
		},
		{
			"cell_id": 1879326238865626154,
			"cell_origin": "client",
			"cell_type": "latex",
			"cells": [
				{
					"cell_id": 1879326238865626154,
					"cell_origin": "client",
					"cell_type": "latex_view",
					"source": "\\subsection*{Declarations}\n\nFlat (tangent space) indices are Latin and curved indices are Greek. Curved indices have\n\\verb|position=independent|, so Cadabra never raises or lowers them by itself, and every\nchange of index type has to go through an explicit vielbein $e^m{}_\\mu$ or inverse\nvielbein $e_m{}^\\mu$. Flat indices are raised and lowered with $\\delta_{mn}$."
				}
			],
			"hidden": true,
			"source": "\\subsection*{Declarations}\n\nFlat (tangent space) indices are Latin and curved indices are Greek. Curved indices have\n\\verb|position=independent|, so Cadabra never raises or lowers them by itself, and every\nchange of index type has to go through an explicit vielbein $e^m{}_\\mu$ or inverse\nvielbein $e_m{}^\\mu$. Flat indices are raised and lowered with $\\delta_{mn}$. The range of the flat indices tells \\verb|join_gamma| that we are in four dimensions, so that products of gamma matrices never produce more than four antisymmetrised gammas."
		},
		{
			"cell_id": 686592541403325356,
			"cell_origin": "client",
			"cell_type": "input",
			"source": "{m,n,p,q,r,s,t,u,v,w}::Indices(flat, position=fixed).\n{m,n,p,q,r,s,t,u,v,w}::Integer(1..4).\n{\\mu,\\nu,\\rho,\\sigma,\\kappa,\\lambda,\\alpha,\\beta}::Indices(curved, position=independent).\n\\delta{#}::KroneckerDelta.\n\\delta_{m n}::Metric.\n\\delta^{m n}::InverseMetric.\ne^{m}_{\\mu}::Vielbein.\ne_{m}^{\\mu}::InverseVielbein."
		},
		{
			"cell_id": 6941258417204097291,
			"cell_origin": "client",
			"cell_type": "latex",
			"cells": [
				{
					"cell_id": 6941258417204097291,
					"cell_origin": "client",
					"cell_type": "latex_view",
					"source": "The gravitino $\\psi_\\mu$ and the supersymmetry parameter $\\epsilon$ are anti-commuting Majorana spinors.\nIndex symmetries are attached to the exact index pattern they are declared with\n(the indices are not \\verb|position=free|), so the curvature is declared and used as\n$R_{\\mu\\nu}{}^{mn}$ throughout."
				}
			],
			"hidden": true,
			"source": "The gravitino $\\psi_\\mu$ and the supersymmetry parameter $\\epsilon$ are anti-commuting Majorana spinors.\nIndex symmetries are attached to the exact index pattern they are declared with\n(the indices are not \\verb|position=free|), so the curvature is declared and used as\n$R_{\\mu\\nu}{}^{mn}$ throughout."
		},
		{
			"cell_id": 2076193461616351723,
			"cell_origin": "client",
			"cell_type": "input",
			"source": "\\partial{#}::PartialDerivative.\nD{#}::Derivative.\n\\bar{#}::DiracBar.\n\\Gamma{#}::GammaMatrix(metric=\\delta).\n{\\epsilon, \\psi{#}}::Spinor(dimension=4, type=Majorana).\n{\\epsilon, \\psi{#}}::AntiCommuting.\n\\psi{#}::SelfAntiCommuting.\n{\\epsilon, \\psi{#}}::SortOrder.\n\\Gamma{#}::Depends(\\bar{#}).\n\\omega_{\\mu}^{m n}::TableauSymmetry(shape={1,1}, indices={1,2}).\nR_{\\mu\\nu}^{m n}::TableauSymmetry(shape={1,1}, indices={0,1}, shape={1,1}, indices={2,3}).\n{\\epsilon, \\psi{#}, \\omega_{\\mu}^{m n}}::Depends(\\partial{#})."
		},
		{
			"cell_id": 2915406222861325085,
			"cell_origin": "client",
			"cell_type": "latex",
			"cells": [
				{
					"cell_id": 2915406222861325085,
					"cell_origin": "client",
					"cell_type": "latex_view",
					"source": "\\subsection*{Curvature conventions}\n\nEvery sign in what follows depends on the normalisation of the commutator of two covariant\nderivatives, so we check that first. With the covariant derivative given above, we want\n\\begin{equation*}\n[D_\\mu, D_\\nu]\\psi_\\rho = \\tfrac{1}{4} R_{\\mu\\nu}{}^{mn}\\Gamma_{mn}\\psi_\\rho\\,,\n\\end{equation*}\nwhich is vN~1.7 eq.~(3) (with $\\tfrac12\\sigma^{ab} = \\tfrac14\\Gamma^{ab}$).\nNote that $D_\\mu$ acts only on the spinor index of $\\psi_\\rho$."
				}
			],
			"hidden": true,
			"source": "\\subsection*{Curvature conventions}\n\nEvery sign in what follows depends on the normalisation of the commutator of two covariant\nderivatives, so we check that first. With the covariant derivative given above, we want\n\\begin{equation*}\n[D_\\mu, D_\\nu]\\psi_\\rho = \\tfrac{1}{4} R_{\\mu\\nu}{}^{mn}\\Gamma_{mn}\\psi_\\rho\\,,\n\\end{equation*}\nwhich is vN~1.7 eq.~(3) (with $\\tfrac12\\sigma^{ab} = \\tfrac14\\Gamma^{ab}$).\nNote that $D_\\mu$ acts only on the spinor index of $\\psi_\\rho$."
		},
		{
			"cell_id": 6486733545005064638,
