		{
			"cell_id": 5318031088668310478,
			"cell_origin": "client",
			"cell_type": "latex",
			"cells": [
				{
					"cell_id": 5318031088668310478,
					"cell_origin": "client",
					"cell_type": "latex_view",
					"source": "All curvature terms have cancelled. Only the terms with derivatives of the vielbein remain.\nThis also confirms that $\\mathcal{L}^{(3/2)}$ has the right normalisation and sign relative\nto the Hilbert action, so the $\\Gamma^{\\mu\\nu\\rho}$ form is equivalent to vN's\n$\\epsilon^{\\mu\\nu\\rho\\sigma}\\gamma_5\\gamma_\\nu$ form.\n\nTo finish, we have to show that these remaining terms cancel against the variation of the\nexplicit vielbeine in $\\mathcal{L}^{(3/2)}$ (vN~1.7 eq.~(10)). For that we need the torsion\nequation for $D_{[\\mu}e^a{}_{\\nu]}$ and a Fierz rearrangement; that comes next."
				}
			],
			"hidden": true,
			"source": "All curvature terms have cancelled. Only the terms with derivatives of the vielbein remain.\nThis also confirms that $\\mathcal{L}^{(3/2)}$ has the right normalisation and sign relative\nto the Hilbert action, so the $\\Gamma^{\\mu\\nu\\rho}$ form is equivalent to vN's\n$\\epsilon^{\\mu\\nu\\rho\\sigma}\\gamma_5\\gamma_\\nu$ form.\n\nTo finish, we have to show that these remaining terms cancel against the variation of the\nexplicit vielbeine in $\\mathcal{L}^{(3/2)}$ (vN~1.7 eq.~(10)). For that we need the torsion\nequation for $D_{[\\mu}e^a{}_{\\nu]}$ and a Fierz rearrangement; that comes next."
		},
		{
			"cell_id": 3240798127517933127,
			"cell_origin": "client",
			"cell_type": "latex",
			"cells": [
				{
					"cell_id": 3240798127517933127,
					"cell_origin": "client",
					"cell_type": "latex_view",
					"source": "\\subsection*{Variation of the explicit vielbeine}\n\n$\\mathcal{L}^{(3/2)}$ also depends on the vielbein, through $e$ and through the inverse\nvielbeine which convert $\\Gamma^{\\mu\\nu\\rho}$ to flat indices. Varying these gives terms\nof order $\\psi^3\\epsilon$ (vN~1.7 eq.~(10))."
				}
			],
			"hidden": true,
			"source": "\\subsection*{Variation of the explicit vielbeine}\n\n$\\mathcal{L}^{(3/2)}$ also depends on the vielbein, through $e$ and through the inverse\nvielbeine which convert $\\Gamma^{\\mu\\nu\\rho}$ to flat indices. Varying these gives terms\nof order $\\psi^3\\epsilon$ (vN~1.7 eq.~(10))."
		},
		{
