			"source": "dL32e:= @(L32);\nvary(dL32e, dvb)\ndistribute(dL32e)\nsort_product(dL32e)\ncanonicalise(dL32e)\nrename_dummies(dL32e);"
		},
		{
			"cell_id": 8457654689019344967,
			"cell_origin": "client",
			"cell_type": "latex",
			"cells": [
				{
					"cell_id": 8457654689019344967,
					"cell_origin": "client",
					"cell_type": "latex_view",
					"source": "\\subsection*{Torsion}\n\nIn the 1.5 order formalism $\\omega$ satisfies its own field equation. That makes the\nantisymmetric part of $D_\\mu e^a{}_\\nu$ equal to the gravitino torsion (vN~1.7 eq.~(11)).\nThe symmetric part is the Christoffel symbol (recall that $D_\\mu$ contains only the spin\nconnection), which we call $C_{\\mu\\nu}{}^q$ so that it sorts before $D$:\n\\begin{equation*}\nD_\\mu e^q{}_\\nu = C_{\\mu\\nu}{}^q + \\tfrac14\\bar\\psi_\\mu\\Gamma^q\\psi_\\nu\\,,\\qquad\nC_{\\mu\\nu}{}^q = C_{\\nu\\mu}{}^q\\,.\n\\end{equation*}"
				}
			],
			"hidden": true,
			"source": "\\subsection*{Torsion}\n\nIn the 1.5 order formalism $\\omega$ satisfies its own field equation. That makes the\nantisymmetric part of $D_\\mu e^a{}_\\nu$ equal to the gravitino torsion (vN~1.7 eq.~(11)).\nThe symmetric part is the Christoffel symbol (recall that $D_\\mu$ contains only the spin\nconnection), which we call $C_{\\mu\\nu}{}^q$ so that it sorts before $D$:\n\\begin{equation*}\nD_\\mu e^q{}_\\nu = C_{\\mu\\nu}{}^q + \\tfrac14\\bar\\psi_\\mu\\Gamma^q\\psi_\\nu\\,,\\qquad\nC_{\\mu\\nu}{}^q = C_{\\nu\\mu}{}^q\\,.\n\\end{equation*}"
		},
